message: Please cite the paper; this repository supplies its source.
type: software
title: "One noisy cellular automaton with an uncomputable noise diagram"
version: 1.3.0
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24147268"
date-released: '2026-11-12'
license: [CC-BY-4.0, MIT, Apache-2.0]
authors: [{"family-names": "Douglas", "given-names": "Seth", "orcid": "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-4708-3252"}, {"family-names": "Mghirbi", "given-names": "Nidhal", "orcid": "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-6534-1118"}]
keywords: ["cellular automata", "probabilistic cellular automata", "noise", "ergodicity", "undecidability", "halting problem", "Gibbs measures", "noise diagram"]
abstract: "Can varying only the noise rate of one cellular automaton encode halting? We construct one computably specified finite-alphabet, finite-range automaton on Z^4 with computable disjoint rational open rate windows. Throughout the nth window, uniform whole-symbol replacement is ergodic exactly when a fixed universal machine halts on n. Halting gives exponential coalescence simultaneously over all initial configurations and attraction from every initial law; nonhalting gives two laws invariant under every spatial translation. Planar rulers and computation boards select the input; three transverse quorum boxes, marked-parent density and interval sensors control errors. The space-time construction gives one fixed translation-invariant finite-range {0,1}-energy interaction on Z^5 with computable rational inverse-temperature windows: halting is equivalent to uniqueness among all Gibbs measures and also gives exponential weak spatial mixing in arbitrary volumes. The earlier Z^6 and Z^7 constructions, their extremal and field-response results, and the Lean verification of Theorem A and its stated corollaries are retained. The Z^4 theorem and Z^5 corollary are not formalized."
